    MsgcoreUtils - JSON and PHP projection of a Msgcore store.

    THE EIGHT CONFIGURATIONS MIRROR Msgcore(2026).vcxproj EXACTLY, and that is not
    tidiness: MsgcoreUtils holds a P3PmsgItem tree open across its whole render, and
    LINKAGE.md's rule is that an offset minted against one copy of the heap does not
    resolve against another. A consumer that links the static Msgcore must link the
    static MsgcoreUtils, and one that links the DLL must link the DLL - so both halves
    of that choice have to exist here, in the same names, or the choice cannot be made.

    WHAT DIFFERS FROM Msgcore(2026).vcxproj, and why each difference is deliberate:

      * Msgcore_EXPORTS is ABSENT from the four DLL configurations. This module IMPORTS
        Msgcore; defining it would flip Msgcore_EXT to dllexport and every Msgcore symbol
        this DLL touches would become an unresolved external it claims to define itself.
        Msgcore_STATIC IS present in the four Lib configurations, for the mirror-image
        reason: an archive links into a consumer that has already absorbed Msgcore's
        archive, and neither half may be decorated.
      * The DLL configurations link Msgcore.lib out of $(WDMSCS_LIB), which
        Directory.Build.props has already pointed at Msgcore\lib\$(Platform)\$(Configuration).
        This project sits BESIDE the Msgcore checkout rather than inside it, so that
        variable reaches it through the Directory.Build.props in THIS directory, which
        imports the library's - see that file for what an empty WDMSCS_LIB does to
        <ImportLibrary>. The ProjectReference below is for BUILD ORDER ONLY -
        LinkLibraryDependencies=false - because Msgcore redirects its <ImportLibrary>
        there rather than leaving it in $(OutDir), so MSBuild's automatic reference
        would look for an import library that is not where it expects.
      * The Lib configurations have NO link step and therefore no reference to
        Msgcore.lib at all. An archive records no dependencies; the consumer resolves
        them.
      * There is no CustomBuildStep. Msgcore's stages its DLL into the shared
        bin\<Cfg><Arch> tree that the surrounding MSCS tree serves from; nothing in that
        tree loads this one yet, and a staging step whose destination nobody reads is a
        copy that can go stale without anybody noticing.
  -->
